<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/units-estimates-and-back-of-envelope">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Back-of-envelope estimation is <strong>the engineer's first superpower</strong>: getting
    within an order of magnitude in minutes, before any detailed work — because it tells you
    whether the idea is possible, absurd, or worth designing. The full analysis takes weeks;
    the estimate takes minutes; and the estimate gates everything, because a design that is
    wrong by 100× will not be rescued by precision. At the Trinity test in July 1945, Enrico
    Fermi — sheltering behind a bank wall, dropping shredded paper scraps as the blast wave
    arrived and measuring how far they were blown — estimated the yield at about 10
    kilotons. The measured value was around 20. A factor of two, from paper, in under a
    minute. This page is that skill, made systematic: decomposition, bounding, unit
    discipline, dimensional analysis, and the arithmetic of precision.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decompose, bound, sanity-check, check units" widget="equationStep" config={fermiCfg} />

  <h2>The Fermi method, run end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Worked example — the classic: how many piano tuners work in Chicago? Never guess the
    answer; decompose it into factors each of which is guessable. Chicago has roughly 3
    million metro residents, say 1 million households. Perhaps 1 household in 5 owns a
    piano (schools and venues add a bit, call it included): about 200,000 pianos. A piano
    is tuned about once a year (professional performers more, living-room pianos less):
    200,000 tunings per year. One tuner, tuning 4 pianos a day, 5 days a week, 50 weeks a
    year, does about 1,000 tunings a year. So tuners ≈ 200,000 ⁄ 1,000 = <strong>200</strong>.
    The commonly cited ground truth (Yellow Pages counts, at their era) is on the order of
    a hundred — our factor-of-2 answer was arrived at in three lines of arithmetic, with
    every factor debatable by a factor of 3 and the result nevertheless reliable. That is
    the miracle and the point: independent errors partially cancel, so a chain of six
    factors each good to ×3 typically lands within one order of magnitude of truth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why is one order of magnitude enough? Because estimation is a sorting device, not a
    measuring device. "Will a laptop battery power a car?" — battery: ~0.5 kWh; moving a
    car takes on the order of 20 kWh per 100 km. Off by 40×: absurd, next idea. "Will a
    rooftop solar array power a house?" — a sunny 30 m² roof at ~20% efficiency yields
    roughly 30 m² × 1 kW/m² × 0.2 × 5 equivalent sun-hours ≈ 30 kWh/day; a typical house
    uses 20–30 kWh/day. Borderline-feasible: worth designing. The estimate did not build
    either system; it correctly classified both, for free, before any engineer spent a
    week on either. Also worth noticing: the tuning chain has a hidden sanity-check — if
    you had concluded 20,000 tuners in Chicago, one tuner for every 150 people, your
    comparison instinct (that would be more tuners than dentists) should fire the alarm
    before any fact-checker does. Sanity against knowns is a reflex, not a step.
  </p>

  <h2>Units, mass and weight, and the disasters they cause</h2>
  <p>
    Units are not bookkeeping decoration; they are the part of the mathematics that is
    load-bearing in the physical world. The SI system builds everything from seven base
    units (metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, candela) and, crucially,
    separates <strong>mass</strong> (kilograms — quantity of matter, the same on the Moon)
    from <strong>weight</strong> (newtons — the gravitational force on that mass,
    W = mg, with g ≈ 9.81 m/s²). The imperial system muddies this deliberately: the
    <em>pound-mass</em> (lbm) and <em>pound-force</em> (lbf) are different units that
    coincide on Earth's surface only because the conversion constant g<sub>c</sub> =
    32.174 lbm·ft/(lbf·s²) ≈ g. Write Newton's second law carelessly — F = ma instead of
    F = ma/g<sub>c</sub> in imperial units — and you are off by that factor everywhere
    gravity is not exactly Earth-standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The price of carelessness is on the record. On 23 September 1999 NASA lost the $327
    million Mars Climate Orbiter because one team's software read thruster impulse data in
    <strong>pound-force-seconds</strong> while the navigation software expected
    <strong>newton-seconds</strong> — a factor of 4.45 injected at every correction burn,
    enough that the spacecraft arrived at Mars on a trajectory too deep into the
    atmosphere and was destroyed. In 1983 Air Canada Flight 143 (the "Gimli Glider") ran
    out of fuel at 41,000 ft because the ground crew computed the fuel load in
    <strong>pounds</strong> where the new metric 767 needed <strong>kilograms</strong> —
    the aircraft glided 17 minutes to a disused airbase and landed on it. Both failures
    were caught by no one, at any stage, because nobody performed a check that takes ten
    seconds: do these units balance? The professional habit is therefore not "be careful"
    (unverifiable) but dimensional checking at every line — mechanical, cheap, and it
    would have saved both missions.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> an estimate that is "only" within 10× is a failure.
  Wrong. The purpose of the estimate is the decision it gates: 10 kg of battery or
  1,000 kg? A 10-metre bridge or a 100-metre bridge? Detailed analysis can (and must)
  follow — but only for the option the estimate allowed to pass. Precision applied to the
  wrong order of magnitude is wasted precision.</p>

  <h2>Dimensional analysis: results without equations</h2>
  <p>
    Units balance not just as a check but as a <em>derivation engine</em>. The method: list
    the quantities a result can depend on, then demand that the units work out. Take a
    pendulum. The period T can depend only on length L, mass m, and gravity g. Dimensions:
    T = seconds; L = metres; m = kilograms; g = m/s². The only combination of L and g that
    yields seconds is √(L/g) — mass cannot enter at all (no combination of metres and m/s²
    makes kilograms cancel). So <strong>T ∝ √(L/g)</strong> falls out with no differential
    equation, and the full solution (T = 2π√(L/g) for small swings) confirms the constant.
    The same machine derives the drag law: a force on a body in a fluid can only be built
    from density ρ, speed v and area A as F ∝ ρv²A — three lines, and you have the
    quadratic drag every skydiver's terminal velocity obeys. And the same machine yields
    scaling laws: strength scales with cross-section (length²) while weight scales with
    volume (length³), so an animal scaled up 10× carries 10× the bone stress — which is why
    giant ants are physically impossible and why elephants have pillar legs. One line of
    dimensional reasoning; an entire chapter of biology, for free.
  </p>

  <h2>Precision: how many digits are real?</h2>
  <p>
    Every measured or guessed number carries uncertainty, and arithmetic must not
    manufacture certainty. The rules of <strong>significant figures</strong> encode this.
    In multiplication and division, the result keeps the fewest significant figures of any
    factor: 3.2 × 4.56 = 15 (not 14.592). In addition and subtraction, it keeps the
    coarsest decimal place: 1.2 + 3.45 = 4.7 (not 4.65), because 1.2's true value lies
    anywhere in 1.15–1.25 and the second decimal of the sum is fiction. For propagated
    errors the working rule: <em>relative</em> errors add under multiplication, and
    multiply by the exponent under powers — a quantity computed from three measurements
    each good to 1% cannot be trusted past about 3%. Ask any spreadsheet jockey quoting
    seven decimals from three input numbers each known to 5%: those trailing digits are
    noise in a suit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond rounding, engineering approximation has a formal toolkit. The simplest is
    <strong>bracketing</strong>: prove the answer lies between a deliberately low and high
    case. <strong>Linear (first-order Taylor) approximation</strong> replaces a function
    with its tangent line for small changes: sin(x) ≈ x (in radians) for small x, so
    sin(0.03) ≈ 0.03 — the small-angle approximation that makes pendulums and optics
    tractable. And <strong>numerical root-finding</strong> makes "solve" mean "converge":
    bisection brackets a root and halves the bracket each iteration — it needs about
    log₂(10<sup>6</sup>) ≈ 20 iterations to go from an interval of 1 to a precision of
    10<sup>−6</sup> — while Newton–Raphson uses the tangent line to jump much closer each
    step, roughly doubling the number of correct digits per iteration. "Precision limits
    and convergence" is the phrase on the syllabus, and this is its content: know how many
    digits your method earns per iteration, and stop paying for more than the inputs
    deserve.
  </p>

  <h2>The estimate-first culture</h2>
  <p>
    The habit generalises far beyond napkins. Estimate before calculating (so the calculator
    has a target to be compared against); estimate before trusting any number anyone shows
    you (a proposed "10 GWe solar farm on 5 km²" fails at the first line — 5 km² of even
    perfect 1 kW/m² sunlight at 20% efficiency is 1 GWe peak, so the claim is out by 10×
    and the proposal needs re-reading before discussion); estimate after calculating (the
    Fermi check on your own answer — did the mass of that flywheel just come out heavier
    than the car it goes in?). The mathematician's nightmare and the engineer's duty are
    the same sentence: "it came out of the formula". A number that has never met a
    sanity-check is a hypothesis pretending to be a result.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fermi estimation as a scientific instrument.</strong> The Drake equation —
    multiplying star-formation rates by planet fractions by probabilities of life,
    intelligence and communication — is a Fermi decomposition with the numbers unknown;
    its value was always the structure, exposing exactly which factor dominates our
    ignorance. Epidemiology's early-2020 estimates (hospital beds needed, doubling times
    from early case counts) were Fermi chains run under emergency conditions. The lesson
    generalises: when the factors are uncertain but the structure is known, decompose
    anyway — the chain tells you where one measurement would pay most.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Allometry: estimates grown into laws.</strong> Biology is where scaling
    reasoning has been pushed furthest: metabolic rate scales as mass<sup>3/4</sup>
    (Kleiber's law) rather than the naive mass<sup>1</sup>, and theoretical work ties such
    exponents to network geometry (transport through branching supply networks). The
    square–cube law is the zeroth-order version; real organisms are constrained optimisations
    of it — bones scaled for load, hearts and lungs scaled for supply.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When estimates fail.</strong> They fail on <em>distributional</em> questions:
    averages hide the tail (a "mean insurance payout" estimate can be right and the
    solvency question still decided by the 1-in-100 event), and on any question whose
    answer is bimodal ("does the reactor melt: yes or no" — a 50% estimate of a binary
    catastrophe is not actionable comfort). Know the estimate's epistemic type: sorting
    device, first measurement, or structure-of-ignorance map. Each is legitimate; confusing
    them is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open practice.</strong> The skill is now testable: interviewers at
    quantitative firms and engineering firms use Fermi questions precisely because they
    expose structured thinking under uncertainty. The training method is volume — one
    estimate a day for a month (coffee drunk by a city, pixels on Earth's photos, bolts
    in a bridge) and the decomposition reflex becomes automatic. The discipline underneath
    never changes: break the question, bound the factors, multiply, check the units,
    compare to something known.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Weinstein &amp; Adam, Guesstimation; Mahajan, Street-Fighting Mathematics; NASA Mars Climate Orbiter Mishap Investigation Board report (1999); Transportation Safety Board of Canada report on the Gimli Glider (1985).</p>

